{pause up}
# Motivations

{.example title="MyModule.mli"}
```ocaml
val foo : int -> float
```

What can `foo` do?

{pause}
- Delete every file you own? {pause} [⚠️ Yes.]{.danger} {pause}
- Open a socket to anywhere on the internet? {pause} [⚠️ Yes.]{.danger} {pause}
- `fork()` bomb your machine? {pause} [⚠️ Yes.]{.danger}

<!-- -->

<!--said-later: AI make things worse, colleagues too :) -->

{pause}
The type says nothing. You cannot [reason]{.em} locally about a module.

{pause up}
## Doesn't Haskell solve this?

The `IO` [monad]{.em} forces you to mark [effectful]{.em} code.

{.example title="API.hs"}
```haskell
putStrLn   :: String -> IO ()
removeFile :: String -> IO ()
connect    :: Socket -> SockAddr -> IO ()
```

{pause}
All three return `IO ()`. Printing, destroying data, reaching the
internet — [coarse-grained]{.em} by default.

{pause}
Plus, this is an **OCaml workshop** :) — we want an OCaml solution,
less radical than the imposed purity of Haskell.

{pause up}
# Capability types

Inspired by [eio](https://roscidus.com/blog/blog/2023/04/26/lambda-capabilities/),
generalized with plain OCaml **object types**.

{.example title="MyCapModule.mli"}
```ocaml
val bar :
  < Cap.fork; Cap.network; Cap.stdout; Cap.random; .. > ->
  int -> float
```

`< x; y >` is OCaml's [object type]{.em} syntax — e.g.
`type shape = < move : int -> int -> unit; area : unit -> float >`.

{pause}
Add `..` — `< x; .. >` — for an *open* row: "at least field `x`,
  maybe more." That's [row polymorphism]{.em}.
<!--  
- Only works in argument position, not in a plain `type` alias like
  `shape` — exactly where capability signatures live.
-->  

{pause}
Now the signature says everything `bar` might do — and nothing it
can't.

We can finally [reason]{.em} about a module — the promise of
functional programming!

{pause down}
{.example title="src/caps/CapStdlib.mli"}
```ocaml
val print_string : < Cap.stdout; .. >  -> string -> unit
val delete_file  : < Cap.fs; .. >      -> string -> unit
val open_socket  : < Cap.network; .. > -> string -> unit
```


{pause up}
