{pause up}
# What is a capability?

{.definition}
An **unforgeable token** that both **designates** a resource and
**grants the authority** to use it —{pause} no reference => no access.
<!--alt: A **communicable**, **unforgeable** token of authority -->


{pause}
Unix already has one: the **file descriptor**. An int that [designates]{.em}
an open file and lets you act on it — you cannot [forge]{.em} one, the
kernel hands it to you.

{pause}
OCaml too with **channels**: `in_channel` / `out_channel` (refined read/write capabilities).

Capabilities aren't a new idea — Unix and OCaml already use them.
This talk just pushes the [discipline]{.em} further.

{pause up}
# Capabilities are abstract (combining) types

Capabilities are deliberately [fine-grained]{.em} — one per syscall and resource;
[object types]{.em} allows to [combine]{.em} them for free, **no OCaml extension
needed**.

{style="display:flex; gap:20px; align-items:flex-start"}
> {.example title="src/caps/Cap.mli" style="flex:1"}
> ```ocaml
> module Process : sig
>   (* abstract: can not be forged *)
>   type fork   
>   type wait
>   ...
> end
>
> type fork = < fork : Process.fork >
> type wait = < wait : Process.wait >
>
> (* combine capabilities  *)
> type forkw = < fork; wait >
> ```
>
> {style="flex:1; display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:16px"}
> > {pause}
> > {.example title="src/caps/CapUnix.mli"}
> > ```ocaml
> > val fork : 
> >   < Cap.fork; .. > -> unit ->
> >   int
> > val wait :
> >   < Cap.wait; .. > -> unit ->
> >     int * Unix.process_status
> > ```
> >
> > {pause}
> > {.example title="src/caps/CapUnix.ml"}
> > ```ocaml
> > let fork _caps = Unix.fork
> > let wait _caps = Unix.wait
> > ```

{pause down}
{.example title="mymodule.ml"}
```ocaml
let restart (caps : < Cap.fork; Cap.wait; .. >) () =
  match CapUnix.fork caps () with
  | 0 -> exec_new_version ()
  | _ -> ignore (CapUnix.wait caps ())
```

{pause down}
The beauty: the OCaml [typechecker]{.em} does all the capability
checking for us, for free — it even [infers]{.em} capabilities!








{pause up}
# But two things are missing

{.outline}
- Where do the *first* capabilities come from? [bootstrapping — `Cap.main`]{.sub}
- What stops someone from calling `Unix.fork` directly? [enforcement — capability rules]{.sub}

{pause up}
# The only door in: `Cap.main`

{.example title="src/caps/Cap.mli"}
```ocaml
type process = < fork : Process.fork; wait : Process.wait; ... >
...
type all_caps = < process; fs; exec; network; misc >

val main : (all_caps -> 'a) -> 'a
```

{pause}
{style="display:flex; gap:20px; align-items:flex-start"}
> {.example title="src/caps/Cap.ml (the secret)" style="flex:1"}
> ```ocaml
> (* not exported in Cap.mli! *)
> type cap = unit
>
> module Process = struct
>   type fork = cap
>   ...
> end
>
> let powerbox : all_caps =
>   object
>     method readdir = ()
>     method fork = ()
>     ...
>     method network = ()
>   end
> ```
>
> {pause}
> {.example title="Cap.ml (entry point)" style="flex:1"}
> ```ocaml
> let called = ref false
>
> let main f =
>   if !called then
>     failwith "already called"
>   else (
>     called := true;
>     f powerbox)
> ```

{pause down}
Every capability is secretly just `unit` at runtime — the `.mli`
boundary is the only thing keeping them apart.

`Cap.main`:
- Callable exactly once — checked dynamically.
- Hands your entry point **every** capability.
- The entry point then **downcasts** (`:>`) to give each callee only
  the subset it needs.

{.example title="Main.ml"}
```ocaml
let () =
  Cap.main (fun (caps : Cap.all_caps) ->
    restart (caps :> < Cap.fork; Cap.wait; .. >) ())
```
{pause down}


{pause up}
# Capability rules

`Cap.ml` alone doesn't stop anyone from calling `Unix.fork` directly
instead of `CapUnix.fork`.

{pause}
Enforce the [discipline]{.em} via a [Semgrep](https://semgrep.dev)
**capability rule**:

{pause}
{.example title="rules/forbid_process.jsonnet"}
```json
{ id: 'use-caps',
  match: { any: ['Unix.fork', 'Unix.wait', ...] },
  languages: ['ocaml'],
  severity: 'ERROR',
  message: |||
    Do not use Sys.xxx or Unix.xxx directly.
    Use CapSys or CapUnix and pass the required capability.
  |||,
}
```

{pause}
No time to explain [Semgrep](https://semgrep.dev) in depth here — I
actually presented it at this very workshop, back in 2021 :) (though
things have changed since — `jsonnet` instead of YAML, different
syntax).
<!-- handle local module renaming (limited) so A.fork detected -->


{pause up}
# Experience report

Rolled out on three real codebases:

- **semgrep** — the original motivations for caps: growing codebase doing
  also networking, executing external progs, and more (junior) contributors
- **mmm** — An old experimental Web browser written in OCaml (Francois Rouaix)
- **efuns** — An old Emacs clone written in OCaml (Fabrice LeFessant)
- **XiX** — Ports of several **Plan 9** systems programs in OCaml: a
  build system, a shell, a windowing system, a toolchain (diversity)

{pause}
Each time: only a few hundreds capability annotations (for projects
around 50k-100k LOC) enough to enforce capabilities across the whole
codebase. Most code is *[pure]{.em}*.

{pause} a handful of
Semgrep rules was 

{pause}
Usually just a few [hours]{.em} to migrate a project. And it's
incremental — migrating forces a better understanding of the
codebase, and documenting things along the way, which makes the rest
of the migration even easier.

{pause up}
# Pros and Cons

{style="display:flex; gap:20px; align-items:flex-start"}
> {style="flex:1"}
> ### Pros
>
> - The typechecker does the enforcement, for free, with type
>   inference — no runtime cost. {pause}
> - [Plain OCaml]{.em}: no extension, no PPX, no new runtime. {pause}
> - Incremental{.em}: a handful of annotations + a handful of Semgrep
>   rules, not a rewrite. {pause}
> - **Uncovers issues buried deep in the call stack** — hard to find
>   otherwise. E.g. a call to `uname` via `Unix.open_process_in`,
>   added by a junior developer, missed in review, and a performance
>   bottleneck. {pause}
> - A quick-and-dirty hack (calling a program, abusing `/tmp`, etc.) needs a new capability threaded through the
>   **whole call chain** — much harder to sneak past review. {pause}
> - Finally a use case for objects in OCaml :) {pause}
>
> {style="flex:1"}
> ### Cons
>
> - Still some [effort]{.em}: migrating an existing codebase, writing
>   and maintaining the `CapXxx` wrapper modules by hand. {pause}
> - `Cap.ml` alone doesn't stop anyone — needs Semgrep, and Semgrep
>   rules can be disabled or `nosemgrep`-ed. {pause}
> - C stub can still bypass everything else, as well as external libraries

{pause up}
# Capabilities and AI coding agents

{pause}
- `Cap.ml` designed *pre*-coding-agent era, for plain software-engineering /
  architecture goals — to defend against junior or corner-cutting colleagues.
  Even **more useful** now, in the AI agent era! {pause}
- A reviewer can trust the *interface* (`.mli`): if it needs no dangerous
  capability, the type system guarantees the implementation (`.ml`) can't use
  one either. {pause}
- Stronger types become a **sandbox** around generated code, not just
  documentation.
- Agents pick up the capability discipline instantly, no coaching
  needed. {pause}



{pause up}
# Related work

{pause}
- **eio** (OCaml) — direct inspiration, but eio is a full IO/fiber
  runtime for OCaml 5 effects; `Cap.ml` borrows just the capability
  idea, independent of eio. {pause}
- Irony: eio itself replaced objects with polymorphic variants in
  2023 — users found them confusing. Its capabilities are also tied
  to one *specific resource instance* (true delegation), vs.
  `Cap.ml`'s coarser abstract permission flags. {pause}
- **cap-std** (Rust) — capability-safe wrappers around the OS,
  removing ambient authority. {pause}
- **Pony** — reference capabilities baked into the type system
  itself. {pause}
- **Haskell** — mtl-style typeclasses (`MonadFileSystem`, ...) or
  effect libraries (`polysemy`, `fused-effects`, `effectful`) can get
  similar fine-grained tracking, at the cost of extra machinery on
  top of plain `IO`.

{pause up}
