# FAQ

{pause}
{.block title="Doesn't Logs need a capability too?"}
No — [ambient authority]{.em}, on purpose. Threading a capability
through every log call would drown signatures in noise for little
security benefit.

{pause}
{.block title="What about time, profiling?"}
Same call: ambient authority, low risk, not worth the friction.

{pause}
{.block title="Won't every signature explode in size?"}
Row polymorphism (`..`) — a function only names what it actually
uses; the rest stays polymorphic.

{pause down}
{.block title="Is this an effect system?"}
Not really. `List.map : ('a -> 'b) -> 'a list -> 'b list` has the
*same* type whether or not the callback uses capabilities. {pause}
Actually an advantage: just close over `caps` in the closure you
pass, exactly like you already do for `in_channel` / `out_channel`.

{pause down}
{.block title="So how does this compare to Haskell, again?"}
Finer-grained — a capability per resource, not one big `IO`. {pause}
But also **less radical**: no need for pure code everywhere. We
don't track general effects, just [syscalls]{.em} — the dangerous
stuff.

{pause down}
{.block title="Why object types, not records?"}
A record needs an *exact* field match. An object's open row (`..`)
accepts anything with *at least* these fields — so a bigger
capability set is automatically a [subtype]{.em} of a smaller one, no
boilerplate.

{pause down}
{.block title="Isn't the space of possible capabilities huge?"}
Not really — real systems have surprisingly few core syscalls. Plan 9
has just **39** :) (`fork`, `exec`, `wait`, `read`, `write`, `brk`,
`alarm`, etc.)

{pause up}
# Going one step further: The TCB

{.example title="src/tcb/TCB.ml"}
```ocaml
(* TCB.ml (Trusted Computing Base), shadows Stdlib *)
let open_in = ()
let open_out = ()
let exit = ()
module Obj = struct end   (* no Obj.magic *)
```

A consumer project wires this into **every** executable and library
stanza in its `dune` files:

{.example title="dune"}
```lisp
(library
 (name my_lib)
 (libraries caps.tcb)
 (flags (:standard -open TCB)))
```

{pause down}
`-open TCB` still opens `Stdlib` underneath, so `TCB.ml` reuses the
same names and retypes them to `()` — killing the dangerous bindings
outright, everywhere, for free.

{pause}


{pause up}
# Attenuating capabilities dynamically

Downcasting narrows *statically*. You can also **wrap** an object to
narrow it *dynamically* — real code, from the `ed` editor in XiX:

{pause}
{.example title="editor/CLI.ml"}
```ocaml
let restrict_caps rflag (x : < caps; .. >) =
  object
    method exec =
      if rflag then Error.e_err "restricted mode on!"
      else x#exec
    method open_in file =
      if rflag && not (Fpath.is_seg file)
      then Error.e_err "restricted mode on, can't read"
      else x#open_in file
    method fork = x#fork
    method wait = x#wait
    ...
  end
```

{pause down}
A "restricted `ed`" (like `rlogin -x`), one wrapped object away —
[runtime]{.em} policy layered on top of the static discipline.


